  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nCurrent H5 analysis shows the 595 dam high centered over the \nmid south into southern Appalachia, yielding yet another hot \nafternoon across our CWA with 3 pm temperatures in the mid to \nupper 90s. Increased southerly flow has ushered in modestly \nhigher dew points, pushing heat indices into the upper 90s to \nthe lower 100s. The synoptic scale pattern remains mostly the \nsame through the end of the week - the H5 high slides just a \nlittle bit further to the east, with mid/upper ridging remaining\nacross much of the CONUS between an upper trough across the \nPacific Northwest and another over Ontario and Quebec. By this \nweekend, the center of the H5 high is expected to return \nwestward, initially over our CWA and then over the Southern \nPlains by late Sunday into early next week. Regardless, the heat\ndome remains firmly in place over the region, with afternoon \nhigh temperatures in the mid to upper 90s (heat indices ranging \nfrom upper 90s to lower 100s) and overnight lows in the mid to \nupper 70s through at least Saturday and likely into Sunday. The \nheat advisory has been extended through 8 PM Saturday. \n\nSlightly cooler temperatures are favored for early and into \nmiddle of next week, although highs in the mid 90s with max heat\nindices in the upper 90s are still likely (NBM 25th and 75th \npercentile for MaxT at MCI is 92 and 97 degrees, respectively, \nfor Monday and Tuesday). Dry conditions are expected through the\nend of the week, with only brief periods of slight chance PoPs \n(15 to 20%) this weekend into early next week across NE Missouri\ntoward the northeastern periphery of the upper ridge.\n\nModels suggest the possibility of an upper trough coming across\nsouthern Canada into the Upper Midwest and the Great Lakes \nregion by Wednesday and into Thursday, potentially sending a \ncold front through our CWA sometime late Wednesday \nevening/night. If this comes to fruition, it would bring a \nbetter chance for showers and storms as well as cooler \ntemperatures behind the front on Thursday, although NBM guidance\nshows a fairly sizable interquartile range for MaxT on Thursday\n(83 degrees and 93 degrees, respectively, at MCI).",
